          #piAs2Functor.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The operation of forming
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              is equivalently the operation of forming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
              of the point inclusion with itself, and hence extends to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-functor"} (2,1)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_1 \colon Grpd^{\ast/} \longrightarrow Grp
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Restricted to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} connected groupoids
              among the pointed groupoids, the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 \colon Grpd^{\ast/}_{\geq 1} \longrightarrow Grp
              of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#piAs2Functor"}
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%282%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (2,1)-categories
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              It is clear that the functor is essentially surjective: for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                      %mo ,
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(\mathbf{B}G,\ast) \simeq G
            %p
              The more interesting point to notice is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              is indeed a fully faithful
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-functor"} (2,1)-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,x), (Y,y) \in Grpd^{\ast/}_{\geq 1}
              then the functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
